Let \(\delta=t-\tfrac12-\varepsilon>0\) and let \(\varphi\) be a compactly supported cutoff. The two coefficients on the left of source (3.9), p. 13, are \[\begin{aligned}
 c_1&=2\left(\frac{2\delta S^2}{3t^2}-\rho\right), \\
 c_2&=\frac1n\left(n-d-\frac{2\delta(n-2)^2}{t^2}\right). \end{aligned}\] They multiply, respectively, a nonnegative \(L^{\nu/2}\) norm and \(\int_M P^{t+1}v^{1-n}\varphi^\theta\). The proof discards the first term and uses the second with \(c_2>0\).

The source defines \(g_i^\varepsilon=P_{\tau\varepsilon\mathbb Z^2}(\ell\bar g_i)/\ell\), using componentwise floor projection, and assembles \[
 A=A_1\chi_{E_1}
   +\sum_{i=2}^{N}\chi_{E_i}
       \bigl[A_i+(g_{i-1}^{\varepsilon}-\bar g_{i-1})\otimes e_1\bigr].\] The assertion immediately before source (6.13) is that the jumps of \(Ae_1\) belong to \(q\mathbb Z^2\).

For \(|z|<1\), \(z\ne0\), let \(S_-(z)=\{\xi:e^{-\xi^2/2}=z,\ \Re\xi<0\}\), and put \[H(\xi)=-\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}
   \int_{-\infty}^{\xi}\operatorname{Li}_{1/2}(e^{-\omega^2/2})\,d\omega.\] For \(x\in\mathbb R,\tau>0,\gamma\in\mathbb R\), the functions on pages 26–27 are \[\Psi(\xi)=-\tau\xi^3/3+x\xi+H(\xi),\qquad
 \Phi(\xi)=-\tau\xi^3/3+x\xi+2H(\xi).\] Suppressing common parameters, (3.41) prints \[
 K_{\rm def}(\xi_1,\xi_2)
 =\sum_{\eta\in S_-(z)}
 \frac{\exp\{\Psi(\xi_1)+\Psi(\xi_2)
              +\gamma(\xi_1^2-\eta^2)/2\}}
 {\xi_1\eta(\xi_1+\eta)(\xi_2+\eta)}.\] Page 70 instead uses \[
 K_{\rm proof}(\xi_1,\xi_2)
 =\sum_{\eta\in S_-(z)}
 \frac{\exp\{\Phi(\xi_1)+\Phi(\eta)
              +\gamma(\xi_1^2-\eta^2)/2\}}
 {\xi_1\eta(\xi_1+\eta)(\xi_2+\eta)}.\] The definition and proof identify these as the step-limit kernel for the same parameters.

In dimension two the source fixes a centered, stationary, isotropic, divergence-free Gaussian field \(b\) whose covariance has Fourier transform \[
 \widehat c(k)=\mathbf1_{\{|k|\leq1\}}
       \left(I-\frac{k\otimes k}{|k|^2}\right).\] The convention uses \((2\pi)^{-1}\,\mathrm dk\) in dimension two. With \(X_0=0\), the diffusion satisfies \(\,\mathrm dX_t=\epsilon b(X_t)\,\mathrm dt+\sqrt2\,\,\mathrm dW_t\). The direction \(\xi\) is a unit vector. The drift amplitude \(\epsilon\) is finite; the sharp comparison constants approach one as \(\epsilon\to0\). Infra-red truncation at \(L\geq1\) gives \(b_L=-J\nabla\psi_L\), with \(a_L=I+\epsilon\psi_LJ\) and \(\mathbb E\psi_L^2=\log L\); \(J\) is counterclockwise rotation by \(\pi/2\).

